# Hosting and origin plan

Decision date: 2026-10-18. This is a proposed shipping contract, not a record of deployed changes. [brief.md](brief.md) remains the source of truth for owner decisions; [hosting.md](hosting.md) describes the current implementation.

## Decision

Keep `<name>.plinth.example` as each app's short, visible address and `app.plinth.example` as the signed-in product. Replace user-controlled top-level documents on the product domain with a Plinth-owned viewer. Run user HTML and JavaScript in a frame on a **separate registrable content domain**, with one stable origin per app.

The current architecture must not open to arbitrary publishers. The infrastructure freeze prevents provisioning that content domain before beta. The available containment on existing hosts is an opaque-origin sandbox, which breaks the `localStorage` apps described in the brief. My recommendation is therefore **no-go for the advertised beta next week while the freeze remains unchanged**. Keep publication closed and serve trusted holding pages at short addresses until the layout below is available and verified. Do not silently redefine the beta as supporting only storage-free apps.

This resolves the decisions we can make without changing the owner's product contract. A freeze exception or an explicitly narrower beta is an owner decision on return; neither is assumed approved. There is no recommendation to relax isolation for two months to meet the date.

## Why today's layout is unsafe

Different HTTPS subdomains have different origins, but share a site when they have the same registrable domain. An app on `notes.plinth.example` cannot ordinarily read the DOM or local storage of `app.plinth.example`; that does not isolate the shared authentication cookies. A cookie scoped to `plinth.example` is sent to its subdomains, and a sibling document can set parent-domain cookies. Depending on provider cookie attributes and validation, user code can read script-accessible session material or attempt cookie shadowing and session fixation. `HttpOnly` protects reads of that cookie, but does not make sibling code trusted. See the [cookie specification's domain and security rules](https://httpwg.org/http-extensions/draft-ietf-httpbis-rfc6265bis.html).

Our edge receives the shared cookie on app requests today. That edge is trusted; the authored page is not. Never forward the incoming cookie to storage, an author-controlled upstream, logs, or an HTML response. An edge check before `index.html` protects initial access, not the authenticated browser after hostile JavaScript starts running. Private-app invitation checks do not make an invited author's code safe.

CORS controls script access to responses; it does not stop every credentialed request or prevent cookie planting. `SameSite` is not a sibling-subdomain isolation boundary. Moving content under another path or another `*.plinth.example` host, sanitizing arbitrary JavaScript, or adding an iframe with `allow-same-origin` on the existing domain does not solve this problem.

## Freeze-period layout and containment

| Address | What may run during the freeze |
| --- | --- |
| `https://plinth.example` | Existing trusted public pages, if any; no authored executable content |
| `https://app.plinth.example` | Trusted product and existing managed sign-in integration |
| `https://<name>.plinth.example/<path>` | Trusted holding/viewer page; retain the name and requested path for later availability |
| Any existing raw-file or alternate edge URL | Deny executable user documents, or enforce the opaque sandbox below; never provide an unsandboxed bypass |

If an internal containment preview is needed, the serving edge must apply this **enforced response header** to every authored document, including direct file navigations and HTML route fallbacks:

```http
Content-Security-Policy: sandbox allow-scripts
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
Cache-Control: private, no-store
```

Omit `allow-same-origin`; grant no forms, popups, sandbox escape, or top-navigation permissions by default. Apply restrictions to SVG and any other document-capable upload too, or serve those as attachments. The edge owns headers; upload metadata cannot replace them. Purge old cached responses before exposing this mode. An iframe attribute alone cannot protect someone who opens the raw URL directly. CSP `sandbox` requires an enforcing HTTP header, not a meta tag or report-only policy. See [CSP sandbox delivery](https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-csp/#directive-sandbox).

This gives authored documents an opaque origin. It blocks `document.cookie` and `localStorage`; IndexedDB, service workers, module loading, fetch, and other origin-dependent behavior also require compatibility review. Adding `allow-same-origin` to repair storage restores the unsafe origin. See the [HTML sandbox rules](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#sandboxing).

This mode is containment, not the promised app runtime. Do not present it as compatible hosting, add a transparent storage shim, or promise persistence. Opaque-origin requests can carry `Origin: null`; do not allow that origin into credentialed product APIs. Sandboxing does not remove cookies from the initial HTTP request, prevent all network traffic, or replace CSRF protection. Keep private documents unavailable in this preview until their complete asset authorization flow is verified; a successful cookie check on the first navigation is insufficient.

If code/header changes are also prohibited by the freeze, leave serving disabled. No DNS, certificate, provider-auth, or production changes are performed by this documentation task.

## Layout to ship once provisioning is permitted

Use `CONTENT_DOMAIN` below as a design symbol for a separately registered domain. Its actual name, availability, ownership, DNS, and certificate coverage are unverified; it must not be configured as a placeholder hostname.

| Surface | Origin and responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| Public site | `https://plinth.example`; trusted public pages |
| Signed-in product | `https://app.plinth.example`; accounts, publishing, invitations, managed auth |
| Short app address | `https://<name>.plinth.example`; trusted viewer, private-access gate, metadata and framing |
| App runtime | `https://<appId>.CONTENT_DOMAIN`; authored HTML, CSS, JavaScript and app-relative assets, served through the edge from private object storage |

Use an immutable, non-sensitive app ID for the runtime hostname, shared across app versions. A rename changes the short address but must not change the storage origin; a different app never inherits an old app ID. Reserve `app`, existing provider/system hosts, and all other operational names from publisher allocation. Match validated hosts against an authoritative mapping; unknown hosts fail closed. Never select an app or construct an authentication return URL from an unchecked `Host` or forwarded header.

The viewer loads the runtime in a frame with `sandbox="allow-scripts allow-same-origin"`. Those permissions are acceptable only because the child is on the separate content domain. Keep forms, popups, downloads and top navigation disabled unless a supported use case is reviewed. The content edge also enforces a corresponding CSP sandbox so opening the runtime URL directly does not shed the restrictions. Restrict its `frame-ancestors` to the mapped trusted viewer origin. The viewer's CSP permits its mapped runtime frame and only product-owned script; it never inserts uploaded markup or executes uploaded JavaScript in its own DOM.

Keep the visible short URL rather than redirecting visitors to the runtime. Map its path and query to the corresponding child path without forwarding auth parameters. Reflect child navigation into the outer URL through a narrow, validated message protocol. Validate `event.origin`, `event.source`, message shape, and the current app/frame binding; never expose a generic proxy for product API calls. Auth sessions, provider tokens, and cookies never cross this bridge. If an app needs viewer identity, return only a server-derived per-app pseudonym, not a global user ID.

The viewer owns title, favicon, canonical URL and share metadata. Public metadata comes from bounded, escaped saved metadata, not executing the uploaded page in a privileged renderer. Private and unauthorized responses use a generic content-free card. Runtime responses carry `X-Robots-Tag: noindex`; public canonical metadata points to the short address. Indexing directives do not enforce privacy.

## Authentication and private file delivery

Keep the existing production auth instance and callback hosts on the product domain. Do not extend its cookie scope, SDK configuration, OAuth redirect allowlist, or trusted origins to `CONTENT_DOMAIN`.

1. The trusted viewer edge validates the managed session and current invitation/access policy before returning any private metadata or issuing runtime access. Sign-in goes through the existing provider integration. Preserve the destination as a validated app ID and relative path, then reconstruct the mapped short address server-side; do not accept arbitrary return origins.
2. After authorization, issue a short-lived, single-use bootstrap grant bound to app ID, viewer, version and expiry. Redeem it at that app's runtime host, then navigate to a clean URL. Never put the product session in the URL. Redact grants from logs, use `no-referrer`, and do not cache bootstrap responses.
3. Redemption establishes an app-scoped content session. Use a host-only `__Host-` cookie with `Secure`, `HttpOnly`, `Path=/`, no `Domain`, and the attributes needed for cross-site embedding. Validate the exact app audience, expiry and access status server-side on **every** private file request, including CSS, JavaScript, images, ranges and conditional requests. Direct runtime navigation gets the same authorization gate.
4. Third-party cookies may be blocked in frames. Test a partitioned-cookie approach in the supported browsers; do not claim universal support from cookie attributes alone. If it fails, show an actionable trusted error and hold that browser's private-app support until a separately reviewed app-scoped delivery mechanism works. Do not fall back to public bucket links or product cookies on the content domain.

Keep origin storage private and reachable only through authorized delivery. Start private responses at `Cache-Control: private, no-store`, including denial and bootstrap responses. Public versioned assets may be cached by normalized host, app version and path; authorization must precede any private byte/cache response. Changing public content to private requires purging all former public cache variants. Revocation blocks subsequent file requests; bytes already downloaded cannot be recalled.

Until the content domain has a Public Suffix List entry deployed in browsers, its app hosts remain same-site siblings. Never issue a parent-domain content session; refuse ambiguous credentials and cross-app grants. `__Host-` prevents sibling injection of the platform's content-session cookie, but does not make all author-created cookies isolated. Request private-section PSL inclusion when eligible and rely on it only after browser uptake. See [the PSL's isolation purpose](https://publicsuffix.org/learn/).

On the product APIs, enforce authentication and resource authorization independently of CORS. Require CSRF protection and the exact trusted initiating origin for browser mutations, reject `null` and authored sibling origins, and never mutate on GET. Avoid wildcard credentialed CORS and suffix-wide trusted-origin rules. Keep downloads and raw uploads from becoming executable documents on trusted origins. Strip author-supplied `Set-Cookie`, redirect and security headers at the content edge.

## Storage, deployment and migration

`localStorage` is scoped to the runtime origin, not the address bar's hostname. Keep each runtime origin stable across deploys and names. Cross-site iframe storage can be partitioned or denied by browsers, and direct runtime visits may see a different partition. Verify persistence in the supported embedded context across reloads and browser restarts; document the tested limits instead of promising unpartitioned storage. If required browsers cannot support that experience, full beta remains blocked.

Existing storage at `<name>.plinth.example` does not automatically move to `<appId>.CONTENT_DOMAIN`. Preserve existing data and inventory any internal testers before rollout. Offer an explicit, user-controlled export/import if migration is needed; never run old authored code unsandboxed on the product domain to copy data. Do not recycle short hosts carrying legacy storage without a deliberate cleanup policy. Existing service workers can serve old documents despite new edge headers: inventory and remove registrations/caches with trusted recovery pages, or retire affected hosts until verified clean.

Retain the current edge and storage providers; this review does not justify a provider migration. After the freeze, provision the content domain, wildcard TLS, edge mapping and private storage access before configuring grants. Put canonical origins, validated host parsing and stage-aware cross-surface URL construction in one shared resolver. Keep credentials in their existing stage-owned stores. Local and hosted preview must use test identities and data, with different sites for product/content isolation tests. Reuse only verified existing preview origins during the freeze; do not invent new preview DNS names or use production auth to compensate for missing staging infrastructure.

## Release gates and rollback

Before opening publication, verify the deployed edge in current Chrome, Firefox and Safari with two apps, two viewers and an attacker-authored app:

- The authored app cannot read/set product cookies, read the viewer DOM, obtain product tokens, mutate product state through CSRF, or bootstrap another app's content session. Test direct URLs, nested documents, SVG, redirects and alternative storage/edge URLs.
- Private HTML and every dependent asset fail closed when signed out, uninvited, revoked or using an expired/wrong-app grant. A preceding authorized request cannot prime a cache for an unauthorized one. Private metadata never appears in signed-out responses or unfurls.
- Cookies-disabled and third-party-cookie-blocked browsers have an intentional outcome. App-local persistence survives reload/restart in the supported context and remains separate between apps and through app renames. Cover modules, relative assets, deep links and browser back/forward.
- Freeze containment, if used internally, rejects cookie/storage access and cannot be bypassed by opening a raw URL. No previously cached document or service worker retains unsafe behavior.
- Test host spoofing, reserved names, normalized paths, cache keys, upload size/file-count bounds and edge rate limits. Confirm runtime headers, certificate coverage and bucket privacy, not just configuration files.

Roll out to internal test apps first. If any isolation or private-file gate fails, disable authored document delivery and show trusted unavailable pages at the short addresses. Never roll back to today's unsandboxed product-domain serving. Keep a separate content-serving kill switch so accounts and the publishing UI can remain available.

## Assumptions and decisions for the owner's return

- Arbitrary publisher HTML/JavaScript is untrusted, including AI-written code and private apps. No application source or deployed provider configuration is present here to verify beyond the supplied hosting description.
- The current routing suggests existing single-label wildcard DNS/TLS coverage, but coverage and reserved hosts still need verification. No additional coverage is assumed during the freeze.
- The freeze is binding. Edge application changes may be possible, but are unconfirmed; the containment proposal does not authorize an infrastructure exception.
- `localStorage` compatibility is material to the intended product because the brief calls it out. A storage-free sandbox beta would be a meaningful scope change, not an implementation detail I can silently approve.
- The content-domain spelling, managed provider's cookie/token capabilities, browser support matrix and private-session mechanism require operational validation. These are release dependencies, not claims of completed setup.
- Recommendation for return: preserve the short-address promise and delay the advertised beta until separate-site hosting passes the gates. If opening next week is mandatory, the owner must choose a freeze exception or explicitly accept the reduced, storage-free sandbox scope and its revised private-app support. This plan assumes neither choice and makes no live changes.
